# ============================================================================
# LibreRun — Environment Variables
# Copy to .env and fill in values:  cp .env.example .env
# Never commit .env to version control (.gitignore already excludes it).
#
# Read by three consumers:
#   1. compose.yaml — variable substitution + forwarded into containers
#   2. backend (local dev) — pydantic-settings reads backend/.env or ./.env
#   3. frontend build — NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL and BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL are baked
#      in at image build time
#
# Minimum to get running — four values, and no provider key (§1 of
# docs/platform/Install.md): APP_SECRET_KEY, INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL,
# INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD and LIBRERUN_STUB_LLM=true. For a real
# investigation instead of the keyless demo, leave LIBRERUN_STUB_LLM at
# `false` and put a provider key in `gateway.env`.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FOUR CLASSES, AND EVERY VARIABLE IS IN EXACTLY ONE (decision L29)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.env` is the SEED LAYER, not a leftover: it is the deployment's
# declared default under every runtime override, and Reset in the admin
# UI returns a setting to the value here. What a variable's class says
# is *why it is in the environment at all*:
#
#   Class 1, BOOTSTRAP — it must exist before a process starts:
#     compose expands it at parse time, or a process reads it once at
#     boot. It can never be a database row.
#   Class 2, POSTURE — it could be a row and deliberately is not:
#     a security posture changes by reviewed deployment, not by a web
#     toggle.
#   Class 3, RUNTIME DEFAULTS — the admin UI overrides it live (every
#     one but OPENAI_BASE_URL). The value here applies until then.
#   Class 4, SECRETS — a credential with no bootstrap role, read at
#     request time by one process, so it lives wherever that one
#     process can reach it. Often NOT this file.
#
# A secret can be class 1: APP_SECRET_KEY, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, the
# INITIAL_* passwords and the per-agent gateway keys are all credentials,
# and all of them must exist before anything starts. That is what makes
# them bootstrap rather than class 4.
#
# Each class opens with a banner line below, and every one of those
# lines is a machine-readable header — which is why the four above are
# spelled "Class N" instead. backend/tests/test_env_example_classes.py
# reads the banners and holds every example file to the rule: each
# variable either settings model, compose.yaml, agents.compose.yaml or
# config/*.yaml reads appears in exactly one example file, under exactly
# one class header. A variable in none of them fails — "configured" and
# delivering nothing is the defect, not the documentation.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE OTHER EXAMPLE FILES
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   gateway.env.example              -> gateway      provider keys
#   observability.env.example        -> vector       the log leg's vendor
#   observability-traces.env.example -> otel-bridge  the trace leg's vendor
#
# Each is loaded by the one service named beside it and by nothing else
# (decision L28), each is optional, and none of them is read by this
# file's three consumers. NO PROVIDER KEY BELONGS IN THIS FILE: it is
# read by the backend, which is the single process every python-package
# agent shares, so a provider credential here is a credential all of
# them have. A key put back on a line here reaches nothing — compose
# passes those names to no service.
#
# Secrets as files: every secret below can come from a FILE instead of a
# variable — set <NAME>_FILE to a path and leave <NAME> blank, and the
# process reads the file (one trailing newline stripped). Docker, Podman
# and Kubernetes secrets need nothing else. Setting BOTH to different
# values stops the process by name rather than picking one. The
# `# or …_FILE=` lines below are the spelling; gateway.env.example
# carries the gateway's own; docs/platform/Install.md, "Secrets as
# files", has the compose overlay and the file modes.
# ============================================================================


# ============================================================================
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# ============================================================================
# Needed before anything starts: compose expands some of these while no
# LibreRun service is running, and the rest are read once at boot. None
# of them can be a database row, because the process that would read the
# row is not up yet.

# ---- Application ----
APP_ENV=development                    # development | staging | production
APP_PORT=8000
APP_HOST=0.0.0.0
APP_SECRET_KEY=                        # Generate: python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
# or APP_SECRET_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/app_secret_key

# ---- Database (local dev only) ----
# Inside containers compose sets DATABASE_URL/REDIS_URL itself, pointing at
# the postgres and redis (Valkey) services with POSTGRES_PASSWORD. These
# values are used when running ``uvicorn`` directly on the host.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://librerun:librerun_dev_pw@localhost:5432/librerun
# or DATABASE_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/database_url
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=20
DATABASE_MAX_OVERFLOW=10

# ---- Valkey, the redis service (local dev only — see note above) ----
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0
# or REDIS_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/redis_url

# ---- Postgres container ----
POSTGRES_DB=librerun                   # Embedded in the container's DATABASE_URL. A deployment created
POSTGRES_USER=librerun                 # before blueprint S2 pins the names its volume already carries
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=librerun_dev_pw      # (docs/platform/Install.md, "Upgrading an existing deployment")
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE is the Postgres IMAGE's own convention, read by
# that image's entrypoint and not by LibreRun. It works, and the password
# inside DATABASE_URL above must still match whatever it holds.

# ---- Named volumes (compose.yaml only) ----
# Defaults shown. A pre-S2 deployment keeps its data by pinning the names
# `docker volume ls` shows, or copies the volumes to the new names once
# (docs/platform/Install.md, "Upgrading an existing deployment").
LIBRERUN_PGDATA_VOLUME=librerun-pgdata
LIBRERUN_REDIS_VOLUME=librerun-redis-data
LIBRERUN_FILES_VOLUME=librerun-file-storage
LIBRERUN_STATE_VOLUME=librerun-state

# ---- Host ports (compose.yaml only) ----
# Every port binds to 127.0.0.1 on the host by default: the backend and
# the frontend take a [HOST:]PORT binding, the others a bare port. Other
# machines reach LibreRun through the HTTPS edge (below), or through an
# explicit 0.0.0.0:<port> here, which is plain HTTP on every interface —
# docs/platform/Install.md, "HTTPS at the edge". compose.sh refuses the
# edge's profile unless both stay 127.0.0.1:<port> or [::1]:<port>.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
REDIS_PORT=6379
BACKEND_PORT=127.0.0.1:8000
FRONTEND_PORT=127.0.0.1:3000
GATEWAY_PORT=8090
VECTOR_OTLP_GRPC_PORT=4317
VECTOR_OTLP_HTTP_PORT=4318
JAEGER_UI_PORT=16686
# Changing BACKEND_PORT, FRONTEND_PORT or JAEGER_UI_PORT: NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL,
# APP_CORS_ORIGINS and TRACE_VIEWER_BASE_URL must follow — scripts/demo.sh
# derives them from the ports when they are unset; by hand, set all three.

# ---- The HTTPS edge (compose.yaml only, the `tls` profile) ----
# Inert unless `./compose.sh --profile app --profile tls up -d --build`
# starts the edge (K blueprint T1; docs/platform/Install.md, "HTTPS at the
# edge") — all but LIBRERUN_EDGE_NET, whose network every start creates.
# The edge also needs NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=/api/v1 and
# BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL=http://backend:8000 below, which compose.sh checks.
#   LIBRERUN_HTTPS_PORT    the one port it publishes, on every interface;
#                          443 is a rootful-Docker publish or a rootless-
#                          Podman sysctl away (Install.md, per engine)
#   LIBRERUN_TLS           where the certificate comes from: `internal` (the
#                          edge's own CA; trust its root), an e-mail (ACME:
#                          the name must resolve publicly and 443 reach the
#                          edge), or `/certs/<cert> /certs/<key>` (your files)
#   LIBRERUN_TLS_DOMAIN    the name browsers use, which the certificate
#                          carries — not `localhost` for a LAN
#   LIBRERUN_TLS_CERT_DIR  the directory mounted read-only as /certs; one
#                          LibreRun alone reads, owner-only
#   LIBRERUN_TLS_CA        a CA of your own, `/certs/<ca.crt> /certs/<ca.key>`
#                          in that directory, which the edge issues from
#                          instead of the root it generated: the way to keep
#                          one root across a restore, since the edge's volumes
#                          are in no backup. Blank keeps the generated root. A
#                          choice on Application Settings wins until "Use the
#                          environment's setting" (T2)
#   LIBRERUN_EDGE_NET      the edge network's first three octets: a /28 at
#                          .0, the edge at .2 (the one address the backend
#                          trusts), the backend, the web UI and edge-control
#                          from .8. Every start creates it, and an engine
#                          refuses a subnet another network holds, so a
#                          second LibreRun on this host, or a network
#                          already there, moves it
LIBRERUN_HTTPS_PORT=8443
LIBRERUN_TLS=internal
LIBRERUN_TLS_DOMAIN=localhost
LIBRERUN_TLS_CERT_DIR=./tls
LIBRERUN_TLS_CA=
LIBRERUN_EDGE_NET=172.16.87

# ---- Images (compose.yaml only; A2) ----
# Every first-party service is built from this checkout and never pulled
# (`pull_policy: build`, #133): a LibreRun release is source only (L37).
# Both lines are BLANK on purpose, and a value written here is an override:
#
#   LIBRERUN_IMAGE_PREFIX  the name the local build takes. Unset, it is
#     `localhost/librerun`, a local name: Docker never pulls these images,
#     and Podman asks no more than the loopback address for a missing one.
#     Set it to name a registry of your own to push the build to: compose
#     still builds them here.
#   LIBRERUN_IMAGE_TAG  which tag. Unset, a local build is tagged `dev`.
#
# The environment outranks this file for both, as it does everywhere
# else (K3).
LIBRERUN_IMAGE_PREFIX=
LIBRERUN_IMAGE_TAG=

# ---- Frontend ----
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL is baked into the BROWSER bundle at BUILD time
# (compose.yaml ``frontend.build.args``). Override before ``./compose.sh
# --profile app build`` so the new value is inlined.
#
# The five layouts, written as "variable -> value" rather than as
# assignments on purpose: a pasted example line would be a second
# declaration of a variable this file declares once, and one variable is
# one line in one class (K4). Both values are baked into the web UI when
# it is built, so a change to either needs `--build`.
#
#   Local Docker dev (no reverse proxy):
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL  ->  http://localhost:8000/api/v1   (the default below)
#     BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL ->  (leave blank)
#
#   Behind nginx, backend NOT exposed publicly (recommended for shared envs):
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL  ->  /api/v1
#     BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL ->  http://host.containers.internal:8000
#     Nginx proxies /  ->  127.0.0.1:3000 only. The Next.js server proxies
#     /api/v1/* to BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL via rewrites in next.config.js.
#
#   Behind nginx, backend on the compose network (no host port needed):
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL  ->  /api/v1
#     BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL ->  http://backend:8000
#     You can also remove the ``ports:`` mapping from the backend service
#     so port 8000 is not bound to the host at all.
#
#   Cross-origin (browser hits backend directly):
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL  ->  https://api.your-domain.example/api/v1
#     BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL ->  (leave blank)
#     Add the frontend origin to APP_CORS_ORIGINS in class [3].
#
#   Behind the HTTPS edge (the `tls` profile, above; T1):
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL  ->  /api/v1
#     BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL ->  http://backend:8000
#     The edge serves the UI and /api/v1 on one https origin, so CORS is
#     moot. The same bundle still works on plain http://localhost:3000
#     without the profile, through the web UI's own /api/v1 rewrite.
#     compose.sh refuses the profile (exit 4) until both lines say so.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1
BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL=

# ---- The platform tenant ----
# The tenant whose admins are the PLATFORM operators. Bootstrap provisions
# the INITIAL_* accounts into it, credentials login authenticates against
# it, and only its admins may edit application-global runtime settings
# (/admin/settings). The schema seeds slug 'dev'; change this only if you
# created a different tenant row for the operator.
PLATFORM_TENANT_SLUG=dev

# ---- Initial users (bootstrapped on every container start) ----
# Read by app.scripts.bootstrap_admin from entrypoint.sh after migrations.
# Both pairs are independent — leave email OR password blank to skip that
# user; the account already in the database is left exactly as it is.
# A pair that IS set is re-applied on every boot: the script rehashes the
# password from the environment, so rotating is a .env edit plus a
# restart. Never logs the password.
#
# AFTER THE FIRST SIGN-IN, BLANK THE PASSWORD (docs/platform/Install.md §4). A
# blank pair is skipped and the account stays, so the value neither sits
# on disk for the deployment's lifetime nor overwrites a password you
# changed in the UI every time a container restarts. Filling the pair in
# again restores it — that is the recovery path, not the steady state.
INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL=
INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# or INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/initial_admin_password
INITIAL_USER_EMAIL=
INITIAL_USER_PASSWORD=
# or INITIAL_USER_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/initial_user_password

# ---- Agents (blueprint B12) ----
# Where filesystem agent discovery looks for agent packages: one
# directory, or several separated by ":" (agents:agents/_examples runs the
# bundled agent and the examples side by side — what the demo does).
# Empty = the agents/ directory next to app/ (backend/agents in the
# standard layout; /app/agents inside the container image). Pip-installed
# agents (librerun.agents entry points) are discovered regardless.
LIBRERUN_AGENTS_PATH=
# The state directory: where agents keep file-shaped state only, never
# configuration — an agent's settings are declared in its manifest and
# valued per tenant in the database since K5b (L32). Compose sets
# /app/data/state on a named volume, so what is kept there survives
# container recreation (blueprint S2).
# Empty (dev mode): $XDG_STATE_HOME/librerun (~/.local/state/librerun).
LIBRERUN_STATE_DIR=
# Base URL at which agent CONTAINERS reach this chassis — advertised to
# them as the run-scoped MCP endpoint (blueprint B13).
#
# LEFT COMMENTED ON PURPOSE, and this is the one shape of line in this
# file that has to be: `.env` is read by COMPOSE as well as by a local
# uvicorn, so a value here reaches the container and OUTRANKS compose's
# own default. That default is `http://backend:8000`, which is what an
# agent container can route to; the dev-mode value is
# `http://localhost:8000`, which from inside a container is the
# container ITSELF. One file cannot carry both, so it carries neither
# and each side keeps its own default — compose's for containers, the
# settings model's for a local uvicorn, which is the same localhost URL
# shown below. Uncomment it only to name an address that is right for
# BOTH, or for agents running outside this compose network.
# LIBRERUN_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8000
# An agent whose manifest hosts it in a container may interpolate its own
# URL variable into `container.url`, one per agent:
#
#   <AGENT>_AGENT_URL=http://my-agent:8090
#
# The name is the AGENT's to choose and the chassis knows none of them
# (L13): compose carries a default for each bundled example, so a
# deployment that does not scan those examples never reads the manifests
# that use them. Set one only to point the chassis at an agent running
# somewhere other than its compose service.

# ---- The LLM gateway ----
# Where model calls go (blueprint S4a). Every agent reaches a model
# through this one process and no other; no provider key is in this file
# (decision L23, L28) — see class [4].
#
# COMMENTED for the same reason as LIBRERUN_PUBLIC_URL above, and it is
# the same trap: a value here reaches the backend CONTAINER and outranks
# compose's `http://gateway:8090`, so `http://localhost:8090` would point
# the backend at itself and every model call would fail with the gateway
# reported `unreachable` on /api/v1/meta. Commented, containers get
# compose's service name and a local uvicorn gets the settings model's
# default, which is the localhost URL shown here.
# LIBRERUN_GATEWAY_URL=http://localhost:8090
# The file the gateway service loads its provider keys from, relative to
# the repository root. The default is what every deployment that sets
# nothing reads; point it at a decrypted copy to run with no plaintext
# gateway.env on disk (K3; docs/platform/Install.md, "Encrypting .env at rest").
LIBRERUN_GATEWAY_ENV_FILE=gateway.env
# How long a rotated agent key's _PREVIOUS value keeps working.
LIBRERUN_KEY_ROTATION_GRACE_HOURS=24

# ---- Per-agent gateway keys (blueprint S4a, D10) ----
# One per agent whose container is started by compose:
#
#   LIBRERUN_AGENT_KEY_<ID>=lr_agent_...
#
# <ID> is the agent id upper-cased with every character outside [A-Z0-9]
# replaced by _ (my-agent-v1 -> MY_AGENT_V1), because compose variable
# names admit no hyphens. scripts/demo.sh generates one per bundled agent;
# `librerun init` does the same for a new agent.
#
# They must exist BEFORE `up`: compose expands them while no LibreRun
# service is running, so nothing inside the platform can mint them at
# start-up. compose.sh derives agent-keys.env from these lines (and from
# the same names in the environment, which outrank them — K3) and passes
# THAT to the gateway — never the whole .env, which holds APP_SECRET_KEY
# and the bootstrap credentials.
#
# To rotate one: move the old value to LIBRERUN_AGENT_KEY_<ID>_PREVIOUS,
# put the new one on the main line, and recreate the gateway. Both work
# until the _PREVIOUS line is removed (LIBRERUN_KEY_ROTATION_GRACE_HOURS
# above bounds the overlap). Two agents may never share a value —
# compose.sh stops with a named error before `up`.

# ---- Knowledge base: vector store coordinates ----
# Not credentials — the index to talk to. The key is in class [4].
PINECONE_ENVIRONMENT=                  # e.g., us-east-1-aws
# Upgrading a deployment that filled an index under the pre-1.0 default?
# Pin that index's name here (docs/platform/Install.md §10) — indexes cannot be renamed.
PINECONE_INDEX_NAME=librerun-kb

# ---- Telemetry routing (compose parse time) ----
# These pick which config files Vector and the OTLP bridge are started
# with, so compose expands them while building a command line, before
# either container exists. That is why they are here and the vendors'
# own endpoints and tokens are not: an env_file is read by the container,
# which is too late. The vendor settings live in observability.env and
# observability-traces.env — see class [4].
#
# Bundled Jaeger viewer: enable BOTH of these together —
#   1. set VECTOR_VIEWER to 1, which makes Vector load
#      config/vector-viewer.yaml and forward traces to Jaeger
#   2. start the profile: ./compose.sh --profile viewer up -d
# VECTOR_VIEWER=1
VECTOR_JAEGER_ENDPOINT=http://jaeger:4318/v1/traces
# Cribl forwarding overlay: setting VECTOR_CRIBL to 1 loads
# config/vector-cribl.yaml for logs and starts the bridge for traces.
# Fill the endpoints and tokens in the two observability files, then:
#   ./compose.sh --profile cribl up -d
# VECTOR_CRIBL=1
# Vendor overlay (blueprint S7a, decision L26): datadog | elastic |
# splunk, or empty for none. Then:
#   ./compose.sh --profile app --profile obs up -d
# An unknown value names a config file that does not exist, so Vector and
# the bridge refuse to start and /admin/otel-status reports it as
# unsupported — nothing is forwarded on a guess.
LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR=

# ---- Source access (K blueprint A1) ----
# Where the source of the version this deployment runs can be fetched:
# /api/v1/meta serves it beside the licence, and the login page and the
# navigation bar link to it. Blank means the default, the public
# repository at the running version's tag, which names the unmodified
# release. If you run a modified LibreRun, set this to where the
# Corresponding Source of your version can be fetched (AGPL-3.0 §13).
# LIBRERUN_SOURCE_URL=https://git.example.com/your-org/librerun

# ---- The secrets store key (K6) ----
# What the backend seals the secrets set in Admin -> Settings with: they
# live encrypted in the database's `secrets` table, never in this file
# and never in plaintext. A comma-separated list of Fernet keys, the first
# one current. Read at boot: a malformed entry stops the backend, naming
# its position. Blank means the store is unconfigured — setting a secret
# answers 503 and each one falls back to its variable in class [4]. Back
# it up with the database: a dump restored without it holds ciphertext
# nothing opens. To rotate: prepend a new key, recreate the backend, run
#   ./compose.sh exec backend python -m app.scripts.rewrap_secrets
# then drop the old key and recreate again. Generate one with:
#   head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 | tr '+/' '-_'
LIBRERUN_BACKEND_SECRETS_KEY=
# or LIBRERUN_BACKEND_SECRETS_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/backend_secrets_key


# ============================================================================
# [2] POSTURE — in the environment by policy
# ============================================================================
# Each of these could be a database row and deliberately is not. They
# decide what the deployment is allowed to do — keep, capture, export,
# accept — and a security posture should change through a reviewed
# deployment rather than a web toggle. The admin UI shows them read-only
# (K9).

# ---- Run modes ----
# Keyless mode (blueprint B14, S4a): the GATEWAY answers every model call
# from its stub provider instead of calling one, so the CI smoke can
# drive a real run with no provider account. Everything else is real —
# the redaction, the span, the token counts, the cost — and reports
# produced this way are fixtures and say so. Read by the gateway; the
# backend reports it via /api/v1/meta by asking the gateway's /healthz.
LIBRERUN_STUB_LLM=false
# Demo mode (blueprint S3): what scripts/demo.sh boots — generated
# credentials printed to the terminal, the stub LLM, the example agents
# and Jaeger on. The only mode that accepts the shipped default
# APP_SECRET_KEY; the backend and the UI show a banner. Never in production.
LIBRERUN_DEMO=false
# Ceiling on one phase invocation's wall-clock budget, in seconds
# (blueprint S4). A manifest's phases[].deadline_seconds applies under
# it; a phase that declares none gets exactly this. Containers receive
# the resolved value in the POST body; their run token expires a minute
# after it.
LIBRERUN_MAX_PHASE_SECONDS=3600

# ---- Authentication posture ----
CREDENTIALS_ENABLED=true               # Email/password auth; required for the bootstrapped accounts in class [1]
BCRYPT_ROUNDS=12

# ---- PII redaction ----
PII_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD=0.7           # Presidio NER confidence threshold
PII_PHONE_REGION=US                    # Region a bare number is parsed as a national phone number of (ISO 3166-1 alpha-2)
# Fail closed (blueprint S4c): when Presidio's named-entity stage cannot
# run, the platform refuses every intake, upload and agent output rather
# than storing text it could only half redact. `true` makes it keep going
# with the regex stages alone — so names, places and organisations are
# stored and exported unredacted, every affected span and record stamped
# and one audit row written. Read by BOTH the backend and the gateway, so
# one setting is one policy; check GET /api/v1/health and the gateway's
# /healthz for pii_detector.state.
LIBRERUN_PII_ALLOW_DEGRADED=false

# ---- Trace export and content capture ----
# The bundled Vector router (config/vector.yaml) receives traces and logs.
# Leave the variable COMMENTED (unset) and containers default to
# http://vector:4317. Uncomment it EMPTY to disable trace export in
# containers (compose uses unset-only substitution, so an explicit blank is
# honored). In development mode (local uvicorn) the local process is
# untraced unless you uncomment and set http://localhost:4317 (grpc) or
# http://localhost:4318 (http/protobuf) to ship to the Vector container.
# Watch what flows: ./compose.sh logs -f vector
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=grpc       # "grpc" or "http/protobuf"
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=librerun-backend
OTEL_DEBUG=false                       # Stream every finished span to stderr + exporter DEBUG logs (verbose; works even without an endpoint)
# The GenAI-convention LLM instrumentations record prompt/completion content
# onto RUN-PLANE spans/events only when this standard OTEL switch allows it.
# Enum values: NO_CONTENT, SPAN_ONLY, EVENT_ONLY, SPAN_AND_EVENT. This
# file sets NO_CONTENT (blueprint S3, gap H3) so a fresh install keeps
# message content out of its traces; a process that has the variable set
# nowhere still falls back to SPAN_AND_EVENT (full capture), the pre-S3
# behaviour existing deployments rely on. Works from this file in BOTH run modes — the
# backend threads it into the process environment before any instrumentor
# runs (a real env var still outranks it). Legacy boolean values are
# auto-normalized (true -> SPAN_AND_EVENT, false -> NO_CONTENT). NO_CONTENT
# is enforced for BOTH instrumentation families: GenAI-convention packages
# read this variable, and OpenInference instrumentors (Anthropic, and
# fallbacks) get an explicit TraceConfig(hide_inputs, hide_outputs) — the
# partial modes (SPAN_ONLY/EVENT_ONLY) act as full capture on OpenInference
# spans. This is the sensitivity switch for the run telemetry plane — see
# docs/authoring/Agents_Design.md "Observability contract" for routing/retention.
# Default OFF (blueprint S3, gap H3): prompts and completions stay out of
# the traces a fresh install exports. SPAN_AND_EVENT captures both —
# scripts/demo.sh turns it on so the demo's trace viewer shows the
# conversation. The other values: SPAN_ONLY, EVENT_ONLY.
OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=NO_CONTENT

# ---- Browser (UX-plane) telemetry ----
# The frontend reports web vitals, JS errors, and page/route views through
# an authenticated same-origin relay (POST /api/v1/_o/e) — no vendor SDK,
# no public ingestion key, and the browser never speaks OTLP; the backend
# translates to OpenTelemetry under service.name=librerun-web. Emission
# rides OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT above (blank endpoint = accepted
# records drop cleanly). The closed schema cannot carry user content —
# docs/platform/Browser_Observability.md is the contract.
# Setting it false is a runtime kill switch: browsers receive 410 and stop
# sending for the rest of their sessions — no frontend rebuild needed.
UX_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true
# Quota units per minute = max(records, KiB) per request, charged against
# the VERIFIED principal (never payload fields). 429 + Retry-After beyond:
UX_TELEMETRY_USER_UNITS_PER_MINUTE=600
UX_TELEMETRY_TENANT_UNITS_PER_MINUTE=6000
UX_TELEMETRY_GLOBAL_UNITS_PER_MINUTE=60000

# ---- Logging ----
LOG_LEVEL=INFO                         # DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR
# Read by the backend AND the gateway. In both, every record is walked
# before any handler sees it, so this is a volume dial, not a privacy one.
LOG_FORMAT=json                        # json | console
LOG_FILE_PATH=./data/logs/backend.jsonl # Host bind mount ./data/logs in container mode (tailed by Cribl Edge)
LOG_FILE_ROTATION_WHEN=midnight
LOG_FILE_BACKUP_COUNT=14
LOG_REDACT_PII=true
# Queue-only logging (blueprint S4): every record is walked on a listener
# thread before any sink sees it, stdout/stderr and the raw descriptors
# are captured, and no handler can be attached that bypasses the walk.
# false = the synchronous pipeline (the test suite's mode).
LOG_QUEUE_ONLY=true
LOG_STDERR_ENABLED=true

# ---- File storage ----
FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND=local
FILE_STORAGE_PATH=./data/files         # In containers this is the librerun-file-storage volume


# ============================================================================
# [3] RUNTIME DEFAULTS — the value until something overrides it
# ============================================================================
# L29's seed layer proper: a database row set in the admin UI outranks
# the value here, and Reset in that UI returns the setting to it. One
# line says "no UI override yet": OPENAI_BASE_URL, a seed no page edits;
# for it the value here is the only one there is.

# ---- CORS ----
# Comma-separated frontend origins. Admin -> Settings overrides it
# (`cors_origins`) and sets a restart-required flag.
APP_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# ---- Sessions ----
# Default session lifetime, and the default for the `session_timeout_minutes`
# app setting the admin UI overrides at runtime.
JWT_EXPIRY_HOURS=24

# ---- Trace viewer (blueprint B5) ----
# Shapes the trace_url field on the runs API, which the run pages render
# as "View trace". Presets: off (default — no link until a viewer is
# really there), jaeger (set it with VECTOR_VIEWER=1 and the viewer
# profile; the demo does), phoenix, tempo, langsmith (needs
# TRACE_VIEWER_URL_TEMPLATE), custom (needs TRACE_VIEWER_URL_TEMPLATE).
# The base URL would be opened by a BROWSER, so it points at the host,
# not a compose service name. Templates may use {base} and {trace_id}.
# All three are overridden live in Admin -> Settings.
TRACE_VIEWER=off
TRACE_VIEWER_BASE_URL=http://localhost:16686
TRACE_VIEWER_URL_TEMPLATE=

# ---- Knowledge-base embedding ----
# The model the PLATFORM uses to embed a knowledge-search query, as
# provider/model. Admin -> Settings overrides it at runtime
# (kb.embed_model); this is the value when no override exists.
LIBRERUN_KB_EMBED_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small

# ---- The OpenAI-compatible endpoint ----
# An OpenAI-COMPATIBLE endpoint to send every OpenAI step to — a local
# server, a proxy, a gateway of your own. Blank means OpenAI's own. It
# applies to chat and to embeddings alike, including the knowledge-search
# embedding above: the point of setting it is that no OpenAI call leaves
# for anywhere else. Not a credential; the gateway receives it from this
# file through compose. No UI override yet.
OPENAI_BASE_URL=

# ---- SSO client identifiers ----
# Not secrets — the public half of each OAuth client; Microsoft's secret
# is in class [4], and Google sign-in needs none. Admin -> Settings
# overrides each at runtime (auth.google_client_id, auth.azure_client_id,
# auth.azure_tenant_id), with the value here as the default; domain and
# email allow-lists are managed in Admin -> Auth Config.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
AZURE_TENANT_ID=                       # Tenant UUID for single-tenant; 'common' for multi-tenant


# ============================================================================
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# ============================================================================
# The credentials with no bootstrap role: each is read at request time by
# ONE process, so each lives where that one process can reach it and
# nowhere else (decision L28). The secrets that must exist before a
# process starts — APP_SECRET_KEY, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, the INITIAL_*
# passwords, the per-agent gateway keys — are class [1] above, which is
# what "bootstrap" means; they are no less secret for it.
#
# NOT IN THIS FILE, and this is the map:
#
#   OPENAI_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY
#       -> gateway.env          read by the gateway alone. Copy
#          gateway.env.example, chmod 600 it, fill in a key for every
#          provider your steps reference. Optional: the keyless demo
#          needs none.
#
#   the log leg's vendor tokens (Datadog, Elastic, Splunk HEC, Cribl HEC)
#       -> observability.env    read by vector alone.
#
#   the trace leg's vendor tokens (Elastic APM, Splunk Observability,
#   Cribl OTLP)
#       -> observability-traces.env   read by otel-bridge alone.
#
# Two files rather than one for the observability legs because each
# process is handed only the credential it reads; docs/platform/Install.md and
# each example's header say why at length.
#
# Copies of any of these that persist beyond the host — an ops
# repository, a backup — are sops-encrypted, and a host that must hold no
# plaintext at rest runs the stack through `sops exec-env` and
# `sops exec-file` (K3; docs/platform/Install.md, "Encrypting .env at rest").
# From K6 a UI-managed secret lives in the encrypted `secrets` table
# instead, with the value here as the fallback.

# ---- SSO client secret ----
# The other half of AZURE_CLIENT_ID in class [3]. Admin -> Settings sets it
# in the encrypted store (auth.azure_client_secret, K6); this line is its
# fallback while the store holds none (L29). Google sign-in verifies its ID
# token with the client id alone, so there is no Google secret to set.
AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# or AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/azure_client_secret

# ---- An in-process agent's own third-party keys (L20) ----
# These belong to the AGENT, not to the platform. Since K8a an agent names
# the keys it reads in its manifest's secrets[] and reads them through the
# secrets capability: this tenant's value, else every tenant's default —
# each set through PUT /api/v1/agents/{id}/secrets/{tenant|agent}/{name},
# a default by a platform admin alone — else, for an agent running inside
# the backend process, the variable here, its name upper-cased. That
# fallback is kept, not deprecated (D35). An agent in a container gets its
# own keys through its own service's environment instead.
#
# No TAVILY_API_KEY_FILE, and that is deliberate: the fallback reads the
# process environment by name, so a _FILE spelling would bind nothing the
# agent reads.
TAVILY_API_KEY=
# The knowledge base's vector-store key: the fallback of the platform's
# kb.pinecone_api_key, which a platform admin sets in Admin -> Settings
# (K8a). Its index coordinates are not secrets and are in class [1].
PINECONE_API_KEY=
# or PINECONE_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/pinecone_api_key
